In this lesson we'll look at how to combine events using "and" and "or," including the difference between inclusive and exclusive "or." Let's start with "and." When you want the probability that two events both happen, you multiply their probabilities together. For dependent events, you'll use conditional probability to keep track of how the first event changes what happens next. Next, let's talk about inclusive "or," which means at least one of the events happens. Here you add the two probabilities together, but then you have to subtract the overlap once, because the overlap is counted in both probabilities and you don't want to double count it. Finally, exclusive "or" works almost the same way, except the two events cannot both happen at the same time, so the overlap is zero. That means the probability of one exclusive or the other is just the sum of the two individual probabilities. Now let's see it in action.